Title: "Builds"
Id: "b8cfcb46-7573-4508-9e30-92f8a7c60664"

Text: "A build views an expression at a stated type. `\\ty@value` is a soft build
and `\\ty@!value` is a hard build. Builds replace the removed `as` and `as!`
cast expressions.

```mlg-fragment
\\set@{x_ : x_ is \\real}
\\set@!n
```

A soft build (`@`) is checked: the checker must justify that the value has the
target type, either directly, through a supertype `extends:` chain, or through
an `Enables: relation:` marked `represents: \\\\coercion`. A hard build (`@!`)
additionally accepts relations marked `represents: \\\\encoding`, viewing the
value at a lower abstraction level.

`@` and `@!` bind the command type on the left to the value on the right. A set
builder after `@` may use `;`-separated specifications, as in
`\\set@{(a_, b_) : a_ \"in\" A; b_ \"in\" B}`. A build that cannot be justified
reports `Could not build ...`.

The related statement forms are `x is T` and the hard variant `x is! T` (or
`x := value is! T`), which introduce a symbol with the same soft/hard view
semantics. A build is also how a `Defines:` value may state its type without
`is`: `X := \\set@{...}` is sugar for `... is \\set`."
Id: "b76683ca-a9fb-48e3-9e79-5156e30e0a96"
